    "https://jobs.omni.fsu.edu/psc/sprdhr_er/EMPLOYEE/HRMS/c/HRS_HRAM_FL.HRS_CG_SEARCH_FL.GBL?Page=HRS_APP_JBPST_FL&Action=U&FOCUS=Applicant&SiteId=1&JobOpeningId=63796&PostingSeq=1": {
      "title": "Associate in Research, 12 Month – Applied Research & Commercialization Hub (National High Magnetic Field Laboratory)",
      "description": "Associate in Research, 12 Month – Applied Research & Commercialization Hub (National High Magnetic Field Laboratory) Next Job Apply for Job Job ID 63796 Location Tallahassee, FL Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular Add to My Favorite Jobs Email this Job Department Applied Research & Commercialization Hub (ARCH) - National High Magnetic Field Laboratory The National High Magnetic Field Laboratory is building ARCH, the Applied Research and Commercialization Hub, and is hiring a physicist who will run its laboratory. ARCH exists to move magnet-based measurement technology from something that works once, in the hands of the person who invented it, to something that runs to a date for someone who needs the answer. The laboratory sits alongside the world’s highest-field DC magnets, a millikelvin suite reaching 20 mK, a maker's space with CNC, manual machining and 3D printing, and the machine shop. Its first book of work is commercial measurement: thermal expansion, low-temperature thermal conductivity and magnetization on materials that go into quantum-computing hardware, delivered to a specification and a deadline. Behind that sits a seed-funded project portfolio and a pipeline of new capability. Responsibilities The Associate in Research runs that laboratory under the Director’s technical direction and works the seam between the MagLab’s research programs and ARCH. Techniques are invented across this laboratory every year. A few of them are worth maturing into something repeatable, and the judgment about which few, and about what maturing them costs, is the core of this job. The successful candidate will spend part of their week designing and running measurements at the bench, part of it deciding what the laboratory takes on next, and part of it in front of engineers from companies who want to know whether the MagLab can answer their question. Run the ARCH laboratory. Own the day-to-day operation: schedule, throughput, equipment readiness, consumables, records, safety. Set up the laboratory from the ground with the Director as it comes online. Design, run and interpret measurements. Take a problem from specification to data to a defensible interpretation, at low temperature and high field, on samples the laboratory has not seen before. Mature techniques into capabilities. Work with scientists and technical staff across the MagLab’s facilities to find measurement methods worth hardening, then take them from a working demonstration to a documented, repeatable procedure with known uncertainty. Scope what the laboratory can sell. Assess a candidate job for feasibility, magnet time, staff effort, uncertainty and cost, and say plainly what ARCH can and cannot deliver. Carry the technical side of customer engagements. Translate a company’s problem into a measurement plan, agree what a result will and will not settle, and report back in terms an engineer outside the field can use. Deliver seed-funded projects. Run assigned projects against milestones, report monthly on progress and risk, and prepare the evidence for the twelve-month Go/No-Go review. Put AI to work in the laboratory. Automate measurement sequences, analysis and reporting. Apply machine learning and current AI tooling to shorten the path from raw data to an answer, and to open up experiments that were impractical when each run needed watching. Mentor, train and supervise. Run the ARCH intern line, and train new staff and collaborators on the laboratory’s equipment and procedures. Co-supervise graduate students hosted from other institutions, whose academic supervision stays with their home group, with scope to take on direct supervision as the laboratory’s grant-funded student base develops. ARCH expects AI to change how this laboratory works and wants somebody keen to push that. Measurement sequences that run unattended overnight. Less time between raw data and an answer. Experiments that were impractical when every run needed a person watching it. The laboratory is being built now, so whoever arrives gets to decide how much of it is automated from the start. There is room in the role to build a student presence over time. ARCH holds no graduate students in its first year. For a candidate who wants them, the route runs through co-supervision of students whose degree and thesis sit with their home institution, widening as the laboratory’s stock of running capabilities and its grant base grow, alongside its links into the FAMU-FSU College of Engineering. The project portfolio throws off problems a thesis can be built on. Qualifications Ph.D.in physics or a closely related field, and post-doctoral research experience or an equivalent period in industrial R&D. Demonstrated expertise designing, building and running low-temperature measurements in high magnetic fields, and interpreting the resulting data. Evidence of taking an experimental method beyond its first working version to something another person can run reliably. Hands-on competence with cryogenic systems, vacuum hardware, low-noise wiring and general laboratory instrumentation. Ability to plan work against a date set outside the laboratory and to report honestly when that date is at risk. Clear technical communication with people who are not specialists in the technique. A strong interest in applying AI to experimental work: automating measurement, shortening analysis, and finding new ways of doing the science. Demonstrated commitment to safe work practices and willingness to support and enhance the MagLab’s high-level safety environment. Preferred Qualifications Experience working with an industrial partner, a sponsored research agreement, a quote, or an NDA. Experience mentoring early-career researchers or junior technical staff, and an interest in supervising or co-supervising graduate students as the laboratory’s student base develops. Depth in one or more of: thermal transport, thermal expansion and dilatometry, magnetometry, electrical transport, calorimetry. Experience with dilution refrigerators and with commercial measurement platforms such as the PPMS. Instrument control and data acquisition programming (Python, LabVIEW) and data analysis at scale. Practical use of machine learning or current AI tooling on experimental data, instrument automation, or analysis pipelines. Proficiency with CAD, 3D printing, basic machining and electronics prototyping. Track record of winning external research funding. Experience authoring standard operating procedures and conducting safety training. Helpful What You Gain at FSU Opportunity to work at a world-renowned national research laboratory. Meaningful contributions to scientific discovery and student learning A collaborative environment that values your expertise and invests in your growth. Contact Info Dr. Alimamy Bangura University Information One of the nation's elite research universities, Florida State University preserves, expands, and disseminates knowledge in the sciences, technology, arts, humanities, and professions, while embracing a philosophy of learning strongly rooted in the traditions of the liberal arts and critical thinking. Founded in 1851, Florida State University is the oldest continuous site of higher education in Florida. FSU is a community steeped in tradition that fosters research and encourages creativity. At FSU, there’s the excitement of being part of a vibrant academic and professional community, surrounded by people whose ideas are shaping tomorrow’s news! Learn more about Florida State University and our campuses . FSU Total Rewards FSU offers a robust Total Rewards package. Visit our FSU Total Rewards website to learn more about our Compensation, Benefits, Wellness, Recognition, and Employee Development programs. Use our FSU Total Compensation Calculato r to calculate Total Compensation options based on potential salary, benefits and retirement contributions, earned leave, and other employment-related perks. How To Apply If qualified and interested in a specific Faculty job opening as advertised, apply through the FSU Faculty Careers site (jobs.fsu.edu). If you are a current FSU employee, apply through myFSU Self Service. Applicants are required to complete the online application with all applicable information. Applications must include education details even if attaching a Vita. This position requires that you have three confidential professional letters of recommendation submitted on your behalf. Follow the steps below to request these letters through our system: 1) After submitting your application, click the Careers link; 2) Click the My References link; 3) Click the Send/View Reference Request button next to the appropriate position; and 4) Follow the steps on that page to send your references a system generated email requesting they submit a letter of recommendation on your behalf. You may also return to the My References link and click on \"Send/View Reference Request\" to see if your references have responded, add additional references, or resend requests. Considerations This is a Faculty position. This position is being advertised as open until filled. This position requires successful completion of a criminal history background check . Equal Employment Opportunity FSU is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. Apply for Job"
    },
    "https://jobs.omni.fsu.edu/psc/sprdhr_er/EMPLOYEE/HRMS/c/HRS_HRAM_FL.HRS_CG_SEARCH_FL.GBL?Page=HRS_APP_JBPST_FL&Action=U&FOCUS=Applicant&SiteId=1&JobOpeningId=63274&PostingSeq=1": {
      "title": "Control Technician 1",
      "description": "Control Technician 1 Next Job Apply for Job Job ID 63274 Location Tallahassee, FL Full/Part Time Full-Time Regular/Temporary Regular Add to My Favorite Jobs Email this Job Department FSU Facilities Maintenance Department Responsibilities Perform advanced troubleshooting tasks for complex building systems such as variable speed drive replacement, machine specific controllers repair or replacement, air compressors control, boiler controls, heat exchangers, fume hoods, lab controls and chiller controls. Perform network diagnostics for FLN and BLN Ethernet and other communication systems. Must be able to set up TECs and BACNet, P1, ModBus, KNX and other networks used in the control systems. Perform all tasks as described in the Maintenance Technician position description including basic mechanical, electrical, control and plumbing tasks. Perform calibrations for sensors, controllers, flow meters and other measurement devices. Coordinate with Siemens onsite support technician to identify and repair components causing system faults. Coordinate with the energy engineer and other FSU team members to resolve complex system problems. Qualifications High school diploma or equivalent and two years' experience or a post high school education and experience equal to two years. A relevant Technical or vocational degree may substitute for experience. A valid Florida or Georgia driver's license or the ability to obtain one upon hire University Information One of the nation's elite research universities, Florida State University preserves, expands, and disseminates knowledge in the sciences, technology, arts, humanities, and professions, while embracing a philosophy of learning strongly rooted in the traditions of the liberal arts and critical thinking. Founded in 1851, Florida State University is the oldest continuous site of higher education in Florida. FSU is a community steeped in tradition that fosters research and encourages creativity. At FSU, there’s the excitement of being part of a vibrant academic and professional community, surrounded by people whose ideas are shaping tomorrow’s news! Learn more about Florida State University and our campuses . FSU Total Rewards FSU offers a robust Total Rewards package. Visit our FSU Total Rewards website to learn more about our Compensation, Benefits, Wellness, Recognition, and Employee Development programs. Use our FSU Total Compensation Calculato r to calculate Total Compensation options based on potential salary, benefits and retirement contributions, earned leave, and other employment-related perks. How To Apply If qualified and interested in a specific job opening as advertised, apply through the FSU Careers site (jobs.fsu.edu). If you are a current FSU employee, apply through myFSU Self Service. Applicants are required to complete the online application with all applicable information. Applications must include all work history up to ten years, and education details even if attaching a resume. Veterans' Preference Certain service members and veterans, and the spouses and family members of such service members and veterans, Certain service members and veterans, and the spouses and family members of such service members and veterans, receive preference and priority, and certain service members may be eligible to receive waivers for post secondary educational requirements, in employment by the state and its political subdivisions and are encouraged to apply for the positions being filled. Veterans' Preference applies to University Support Personnel System (USPS) positions only. IMPORTANT: To claim Veterans' Preference, select “yes” to the question on the application questionnaire and upload a DD-214 (or equivalent), the Veterans' Preference Certification form, and other documentation if applicable with your online application before midnight of the position closing date. Click the links for Veterans’ Preference eligibility requirements and Veterans’ Preference required documentation (PDF) , or call FSU Human Resources at (850) 644-6034 . Considerations This is an USPS (University Support Personnel System) position. This position requires successful completion of a criminal history background check . This position is being re-advertised. Previous applicants need not apply. Equal Employment Opportunity FSU is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer. Apply for Job"
